---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.svg";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="Four overlapping document icons in flat gold and black line art, each labelled with a near-duplicate filename: Copy of report (2).docx, Untitled-3.docx, final_v2.docx, final_FINAL_revised2.docx. The front one is circled with a stamp reading CURRENT."
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <section class="opening-statement" aria-label="Opening statement">
    <p class="opening-statement-kicker">Opening statement</p>
    <p>There is no such thing as &ldquo;the file.&rdquo;</p>
    <p>
      There is the attachment you emailed. The copy in Downloads. The one you
      renamed. The autosave. The version your collaborator edited. The PDF
      you exported. The PDF they annotated. The copy stored in Drive. The
      copy Drive created when syncing failed.
    </p>
    <p>This course studies what happens next.</p>
  </section>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    Every week hands you a folder that already has too many files in it.
    You inspect one (your own, or one staged to be misleading) and argue
    for which copy counts. You reconstruct an edit history from timestamps
    and metadata that contradict each other. You merge two versions of the
    same document and write down what you cut, and why. You run one file
    through a chain of format conversions and mark the exact step where it
    stopped being itself. Twelve weeks, four pieces of evidence, no essays.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    For anyone who has three files called <code>final</code> and no reliable
    way to say which one to send. No programming background is assumed.
    Comfort with ambiguity is not assumed either. The course does not
    resolve it. Most weeks end on a decision, not an answer.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title="Syllabus" href="/syllabus/">
      <p>The thesis, the twelve-week schedule, and how the four assessments fit it.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Lectures" href="/lectures/">
      <p>The weekly argument, one core question at a time, with slides.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>
        The weekly hands-on exercise: files to inspect, timelines to
        reconstruct, conflicts to resolve.
      </p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>What is marked, what it is worth, and when it is due.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who teaches the course and how to reach them.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .opening-statement {
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
    border-inline-start: 4px solid var(--at-primary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
  }

  .opening-statement p {
    margin-block: 0.75rem;
  }

  .opening-statement p:first-of-type {
    margin-block-start: 0;
  }

  .opening-statement-kicker {
    margin-block-end: 1rem;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    font-family: ui-monospace, "Courier New", monospace;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }
</style>
